1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 21: dishes

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:23,325
YA-NEC course, lesson 21: dishes. Prime focus, horns and feeds, offset dish: three calculators of the DISHES family.

3
00:00:23,375 --> 00:00:33,960
Prime-focus dish: 23 cm, 3 m dish, f/D 0.42, 10 dB edge taper. Everything is recalculated while you type.

4
00:00:34,010 --> 00:00:49,687
Summary: 30.2 dBi, 64 % efficiency, -3 dB beamwidth 5.04 degrees. The budget shows where it is lost: spillover 91 %, illumination 90 %, feed and strut blockage 94 %.

5
00:00:49,737 --> 00:00:59,354
Surface with the Ruze formula (3 mm RMS) and mesh with Mumford: at 23 cm they cost very little. The sources are on the Fonti page.

6
00:00:59,404 --> 00:01:22,749
Let's try f/D 0.38: the deeper dish changes spillover and taper, and the gain updates at once.

7
00:01:22,799 --> 00:01:33,082
Lobes: pattern from aperture integration, with and without feed blockage; below, the beamwidth compared with the formulas in the literature.

8
00:01:33,132 --> 00:01:44,549
Mechanics: scale cross section with focus, depth and rim angle, then weights, centre of gravity and counterweight. In the warnings, wind and ice on the mesh.

9
00:01:44,599 --> 00:01:59,600
Bands and EME: the same dish on 23, 13, 6 and 3 cm. Over 40 dBi on 6 cm, but on 3 cm a 3 mm surface is no longer enough: the Ruze column says so.

10
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:11,735
Horns and feeds: coffee-can, VE4MA, Chaparral, W2IMU, conical and pyramidal. Here the W2IMU dual-mode on 1296 MHz.

11
00:02:11,785 --> 00:02:22,123
Pattern: E and H planes, -10 dB at 54 degrees off axis, 10.5 dBi gain. The dashed lines are the level wanted at the dish edge.

12
00:02:22,173 --> 00:02:32,118
On the dish: feed efficiency versus f/D. With this horn the best is around f/D 0.50; the phase centre tells where to put the mouth.

13
00:02:32,168 --> 00:02:41,452
From the NEC model page the horn becomes a file to open in YA-NEC, and the dish can use its pattern with the NEC feed button.

14
00:02:41,502 --> 00:02:53,658
Offset dish: a slice of a paraboloid with the feed out of the beam. No blockage, but asymmetric illumination.

15
00:02:53,708 --> 00:03:06,039
10 GHz, 1 x 1.1 m dish: 38.9 dBi, 65 % efficiency, 1.92 degree beam. Feed blockage 100 %: no loss.

16
00:03:06,089 --> 00:03:20,520
Lobes in the H and V planes, and Geometry with the templates to build the dish: profile and rims relative to the focus.

17
00:03:20,570 --> 00:03:30,520
With circular polarization the offset beam moves (squint, Adatia and Rudge): on 10 GHz 0.15 degrees per sense, to keep in mind for EME.

18
00:03:30,570 --> 00:03:40,429
Dishes: prime focus, horns and offset in the same engine, with sources and warnings. Next lesson: real terrain. 73 de IC8TEM
